# Normalize all text to LF in history AND force LF in the working tree, so the editor / odinfmt (both
# LF, per .editorconfig and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`) match what git checks out - even when
# core.autocrlf=true, which Git for Windows sets in its system config. eol=lf overrides autocrlf.
#
# Keep this in sync with .editorconfig's `end_of_line` and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`. odinfmt does
# NOT read .editorconfig, so without the odinfmt.json setting it writes CRLF on Windows and re-dirties
# the tree after every `just format` against this LF index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch is the exception: CRLF is what cmd.exe natively expects, and LF-only batch has known
# failure modes around labels and multi-line blocks. src/build.cmd and src/build-v3.cmd were being
# checked out LF by the rule above - they happen to work, but this is not a bet worth carrying on the
# scripts that build the DDS library. Going LF -> CRLF cannot break a batch file; only the reverse can.
#
# Nothing needs committing for this. The index keeps LF either way (the clean filter strips CR on the
# way in); `eol=crlf` only changes what the smudge filter writes into the working directory. Git will
# NOT rewrite a file that is already sitting there, so an existing checkout stays LF until the file is
# re-checked-out (`rm src/build.cmd && git checkout -- src/build.cmd`), and `git status` reads clean
# before and after. `git add --renormalize` is the wrong tool here - it operates on the index, which is
# already correct.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never normalize these. `text=auto` guesses by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes, which
# is a heuristic and not a guarantee - this repository already shows it going both ways on the SAME
# format: external/dds/doc/Par Calculation.rtf is auto-detected binary while the other three .rtf files
# are not, purely because of where NUL bytes happen to land. The prebuilt libraries and the vendored
# Office/PDF documents are the files where guessing wrong means silent corruption, so they say so
# explicitly rather than relying on the guess.
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.lib binary
*.dll binary
*.obj binary
*.o binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.a binary
*.bc binary
*.spall binary
*.pdf binary
*.rtf binary
*.mht binary
*.thmx binary

# The vendored upstream DDS source (see external/dds/VENDORED.md) is 30 headers and 27 .cpp files
# against this project's 23 .odin, so GitHub's language bar reports a C++ repository unless it is told
# otherwise. `vendored` rather than `generated`: it is hand-written, just not ours.
#
# The trailing `**` is load-bearing. gitattributes borrows gitignore's pattern syntax but not its
# directory semantics: a pattern matching a directory does NOT recursively cover the paths inside it,
# so a bare `external/dds/` would apply to nothing at all.
external/dds/** linguist-vendored=true
